  "query_preview": "..\nHigh pressure moving out of eastern Montana this morning with\nupper ridge building into the northern Rockies. With the\ndevelopment of a chinook arch and lee trough, temperatures will be\nwarming over the next few days. Pressure gradient between the high\nand the trough could bring winds up close to Lake Wind Criteria\nbriefly midday, but will be fairly marginal and short lived. \n\nShortwave ejecting from the eastern Pacific trough will flatten\nout the ridge over the weekend, for a drop in temperatures on\nSunday behind a cold front. \n\nUpper flow will turn increasingly southeast early next week with\nan upper low digging south off the west coast. By midweek flow\nwill split with the upper low cutting off from the Canadian\nnorthern near zonal flow. Next chance for precipitation appears to\nbe late next week as the closed low moves onshore and begins to\nopen up. This will allow it to phase with a northern stream trough\nand pull Pacific moisture northward through the Great Basin.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\n- There is high confidence in conditions remaining largely benign\n  throughout the forecast period.\n\n- No deviations from the NBM.",
